<%# The report form, in the footer's popup. ONE definition, because there is only
    ONE place an operator reports from: the footer. The failure path renders the
    error IN this popup (the controller answers JSON) rather than navigating away,
    so a failed filing never costs the reporter their words — which is the whole
    risk of a report box wired to a third-party API.

    The three boxes are deliberate and not one textarea: "what happened" and
    "what I expected" are the two halves of every useful bug report, and a single
    box cannot prompt for the second one. The kind select is a <select> rather
    than radio pills because it is a value the CONTROLLER reads, not a layout
    choice.

    Every name is `report[...]`, and every id below is one the popup's submit
    handler reads — a writer for a control this form never renders would post a
    value nobody chose. The submit button carries data-report-submit so the
    handler can disable it during the call, and the result lands in
    [data-report-result]. %>


<%# Unconfigured is stated BEFORE the boxes, not as a disabled submit: an
    operator must be able to write the report down even when the box cannot file
    it, and a dead button is how a good description gets lost. %>
<% unless bug_reporting_configured? %>
  <p class="report-unconfigured status-pill status-fail">
    Filing is not configured on this box (no <span class="mono">CHOPSHOP_GITHUB_TOKEN</span>),
    so this will be kept on screen rather than sent.
  </p>
<% end %>

<%# `scope: :report` IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness. form_with derives its scope
    from the ROUTE NAME when none is given, so a form built only with
    `url: bug_reports_path` posts `bug_report[summary]`, `bug_report[happened]`
    -- and the controller reads `params[:report]`, so every field arrives EMPTY
    and the filed issue reads "_(not given)_" in all three boxes. It is invisible
    to a test that posts params by hand (which is how this was shipped once) and
    only shows up in an issue filed by a real browser. Pinned by a test that
    asserts the rendered input NAMES. %>
<%= form_with url: bug_reports_path, method: :post, local: true, scope: :report,
              class: "report-form", id: "bug-report-form",
              # The hook the submit handler matches on. It MUST be written
              # `report_form:`, not `report: "form"`: the `data:` hash turns a
              # symbol into `data-<symbol>`, so `report:` emits `data-report` and
              # the handler's `closest('[data-report-form]')` never matches -- the
              # browser then does a NATIVE submit, the operator is navigated to
              # a JSON body, and every word they typed is gone. That is exactly
              # the failure this form exists to prevent, from a typo.
              data: { report_form: true } do |f| %>
  <%# The page the reporter is ON, filled in by the popup at open time. The
      controller prefers it over the Referer because the submit is a fetch with
      no document behind it, and `request.path` would otherwise report this
      endpoint ("/reports") as the page the problem happened on -- which tells a
      triager nothing at all. A hidden field, not JS-built FormData, so the
      no-JavaScript submission carries a page too. %>
  <%= f.hidden_field :page, id: "report_page", value: "" %>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="report_kind">What kind of report is this?</label>
    <%= f.select :kind,
                 BugReportsController::KINDS.map { |value, label| [label, value] },
                 { selected: "bug" }, id: "report_kind" %>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="report_summary">One-line summary</label>
    <%= f.text_field :summary, id: "report_summary", maxlength: 220,
                     placeholder: "The page the pipeline times out on" %>
    <p class="hint">Becomes the issue title &mdash; say where and what.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="report_happened">What happened?</label>
    <%= f.text_area :happened, id: "report_happened", rows: 3,
                    placeholder: "What you did, and what the studio did back." %>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="report_expected_behaviour">What did you expect?</label>
    <%= f.text_area :expected_behaviour, id: "report_expected_behaviour", rows: 2,
                    placeholder: "The other half of the report. Even one line helps." %>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="report_details">Anything else</label>
    <%= f.text_area :details, id: "report_details", rows: 2,
                    placeholder: "Steps to reproduce, a suggestion, anything unsaid." %>
  </div>

  <%# What the operator does NOT have to supply, said out loud rather than
      silently appended: the page, the account, the version and the browser
      travel with the report because they are facts the reporter cannot see and
      would otherwise be chased for by email. %>
  <p class="report-meta mono">
    Sent with it: this page, your account handle, the studio version, your browser. Nothing else.
  </p>

  <%# aria-live so a screen reader hears the outcome; it starts empty rather than
      hidden, because an empty live region is not announced when it fills. %>
  <p class="report-result" data-report-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

  <div class="report-actions">
    <button type="submit" class="head-action" data-report-submit>File this report</button>
  </div>
<% end %>
